# College Practice Test — Interview Question Types

This repository documents common categories of interview/practice-test questions typically encountered in college coding assessments and campus placement drives.

## 1. Array-Based Questions
Problems involving traversal, searching, sorting, or manipulating arrays.
- Examples: finding max/min, filtering elements based on a condition, reversing an array, two-sum, rotating arrays.
- Skills tested: loops, conditionals, indexing.

## 2. String Manipulation
Problems that test string parsing, formatting, and manipulation.
- Examples: palindrome check, anagram check, string reversal, word frequency count, pattern matching.
- Skills tested: string methods, character arrays, regex basics.

## 3. Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)
Problems requiring classes, objects, inheritance, and polymorphism.
- Examples: designing a class hierarchy (e.g., Vehicle → Car, Bike), implementing interfaces, encapsulation-based problems.
- Skills tested: class design, constructors, method overriding/overloading.

## 4. Sorting & Searching
Problems requiring implementation or application of sorting/searching algorithms.
- Examples: implementing bubble/merge/quick sort, binary search, finding kth largest element.
- Skills tested: algorithmic complexity (time/space), recursion.

## 5. Recursion & Backtracking
Problems solved using recursive logic.
- Examples: factorial, Fibonacci, permutations, subsets, N-Queens.
- Skills tested: base cases, recursive tree thinking, stack behavior.

## 6. Data Structures
Problems involving stacks, queues, linked lists, trees, and graphs.
- Examples: implementing a stack using arrays, reversing a linked list, tree traversals (in-order/pre-order/post-order), BFS/DFS on graphs.
- Skills tested: pointer/reference manipulation, structural thinking.

## 7. Dynamic Programming (DP)
Problems requiring optimal substructure and overlapping subproblem techniques.
- Examples: knapsack problem, longest common subsequence, coin change, climbing stairs.
- Skills tested: memoization, tabulation, state transition design.

## 8. Simulation / Real-World System Design (Mini)
Problems that simulate a real-world system using basic data structures, similar to the Netflix Movie Recommendation System.
- Examples: library management system, movie recommendation filter, employee payroll calculator, inventory tracker.
- Skills tested: parsing structured input, applying business logic, formatted output.

## 9. Math & Logic-Based Problems
Problems testing mathematical reasoning and logical thinking.
- Examples: prime number checks, GCD/LCM, number pattern printing, matrix operations.
- Skills tested: mathematical formulas, nested loops, edge case handling.

## 10. Input/Output Handling Edge Cases
Many college practice tests specifically test whether a program handles input parsing correctly.
- Examples: reading N lines of mixed data types, handling trailing spaces, parsing space-separated values into arrays.
- Skills tested: Scanner/BufferedReader usage, type casting, input validation.

---

## Repository Structure (Suggested)
```
college-practice-tests/
  ├── README.md                     (this file)
  ├── arrays/
  ├── strings/
  ├── oop/
  ├── sorting-searching/
  ├── recursion/
  ├── data-structures/
  ├── dynamic-programming/
  ├── simulation-systems/
  └── math-logic/
```

Each folder can contain individual problems with their own README.md (question) and solution file (e.g., Solution.java).
